\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Define the Euler up/down numbers $E_n$ for integers $n\ge0$ by
\[
\sum_{n\ge0} E_n\frac{z^n}{n!}=\sec z+\tan z.
\]
For $q\in\mathbb N$ this sequence modulo $q$ is eventually periodic. Its minimal preperiod is
\[
s(q)=\min\{s\in\mathbb Z_{\ge0}:\exists d\in\mathbb N\ \forall n\ge s,\ E_{n+d}\equiv E_n\pmod q\},
\]
and its minimal eventual period is
\[
d(q)=\min\{d\in\mathbb N:\forall n\ge s(q),\ E_{n+d}\equiv E_n\pmod q\}.
\]
The prime-level periods are $d(p)=p-1$ for $p\equiv1\pmod4$ and $d(p)=2(p-1)$ for $p\equiv3\pmod4$.\par\textit{Historical context.} These are the two parts of Ramassamy's original conjecture. A 2026 paper reports a proof of the period formula and the counterexample $s(5^5)=4<5$ to the preperiod formula. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.27058}{[S3]}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every odd prime $p$, does $s(p^k)=k$ hold for every integer $k\ge1$, and does $d(p^k)=p^{k-1}d(p)$ hold for every integer $k\ge2$? \href{https://amj.math.stonybrook.edu/html-articles/Files-2015-2024/18-79/index.html}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Determine the exact starting point and period of the Euler-number sequence modulo every odd prime power; the original two predictions are recorded with their later source context.

\subsection{Sources}
